211:<p>Of the 105: 47 are arrays, 25 of them waiting for the library change that batch 13 built and held back (below); 30 are in bodies of self-typed traits and wait for a judgement; 12 wait for other decisions of the curator's; 12 need <code>where</code> clauses on both paths; 3 are clause forms with no written element type, which the checker refuses (row 425); 1 varies with the order in which the checker asks its questions (row 488). The ranges are no longer a group: batch 11 cleared 28 range sites and batch 12 nine.</p>
313:<h2><span class="num">5</span>What the revival changed in Fortress itself</h2>
364:<ol><li>A call chooses among overloaded declarations by the run-time types of all its arguments.</li><li>Functional methods: a method with <code>self</code> among its parameters is called as <code>f(x)</code>.</li><li>Traits and objects, no classes.</li><li>Static parameters, written <code>[\T\]</code>.</li><li>Numbers are side by side, not a tower.</li><li>Loops and reductions are library code.</li><li>Juxtaposition is an operator, and spaces count.</li><li>What the specification describes and nobody built.</li></ol>
448:<li>Resolution: such a parameter now takes its bound, as the 2019 paper says. The compiled program no longer crashes at load for this reason. Under walk, since batch 11, a parameter whose bound names itself, such as <code>SUM</code>'s, stays open instead and every value passes it, because walk has no static types to take the element type from. Four rarer cases still get <code>Bottom</code> under walk.
536:<p>Two answers are wrong for both the team and us: integer types that wrap by themselves (the specification wraps only with its special operators), and an <code>Any</code> type that does not hold tuples.</p>
